  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/c1/Avengers_Vol_1_2.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "<i>Fair Warning</i>:&nbsp; the plot summary below is full of \"<i>what?</i>\" and \"<i>you gotta be kidding me!</i>\" type knee-jerk reactions on my part, <i>far more</i> than ever before because it's so ridiculous.&nbsp; Please forgive the largely-involuntary real-time reactions.&nbsp; I guess this is a hint of the type of grade this story will be getting, <i>heh</i>...\n\nWe open up in the \"library of Anthony Stark, millionaire weapons manufacturer.\"&nbsp; Nice to know that from the get-go, Tony Stark provides the Avengers' funding.&nbsp; Once Hank and Jan appear (continuing their blatant pill popping from the last issue of <i>Tales to Astonish</i> - <i>yeesh</i>), they sit down to, as Iron Man points out, \"get to know each other better.\"\n\nMeanwhile we see the Space Phantom arriving on Earth, shaped as a \"strange egg-like object\" where he plainly indicates it's been \"weeks of faster-than-light travel.\"&nbsp; He lands on Earth and then <i>immediately</i> sets out to \"find the Avengers and begin Phase Two\" - <i>naturally</i> he's arrived on Earth to destroy the Avengers so that \"no power on Earth can stop the total invasion by my people!\"&nbsp; (<i>Oh boy, here we go again with yet <b>another</b> alien invasion force</i>!)&nbsp; But more to the point, how in the world has the Space Phantom even <i>heard</i> of the Avengers, who <i>just formed</i> - you'd think that if it's taken him weeks of faster-than-light travel, he'd arrive on Earth with little to no information about current events on Earth, particularly the newly-formed group, even though he says he's been observing them \"for months from space\" (huh?&nbsp; They <i>just</i> formed!).&nbsp; <i>Sigh</i> - sometimes these inconsistencies are just too obvious...&nbsp; I'll just have to overlook this as I continue reading (but <i>ugh</i>...).&nbsp; Heh, you <i>do</i> have to at least love those eyeballs that Kirby draws him with - kinda creepy - pointed hair and pointed ears to boot!\n\nWe learn the Space Phantom can take on the form of anyone, while at the same time banishing that person to \"limbo.\"&nbsp; He takes the form of the Hulk and starts a brawl between the Avengers to stir up trouble.&nbsp; But later, he's out on the street and is approached by Rick Jones - <i>what</i>?&nbsp; In the previous issue, Rick Jones and the Teen Brigade were in their headquarters, \"in the Southwest, halfway across the country.\"&nbsp; What's he doing in New York?&nbsp; <i>Sigh</i>...\n\nThrough Rick Jones, the Space Phantom learns that the Hulk has another identity (which the text shows as \"Doctor Don Blake\" in an obvious no-prize-worthy goof - <i>ha</i>), which he apparently did not know, but he pointed out a few pages earlier that he knew Iron Man was really Tony Stark from his \"months\" of observations from space - <i>seriously</i>?&nbsp; How could he have not learned that the Hulk too had another identity?&nbsp; (This is really getting to be a mess on the consistency front!)\n\nThe \"Hulk\" then destroys some of Stark's missile equipment, and Iron Man soon confronts him.&nbsp; During the battle, the Space Phantom assumes another shape, leaving Iron Man now facing the <i>real</i> Hulk, but not realizing the change.&nbsp; At the same time, Rick Jones has \"reached the nearest member of his nation-wide Teen Brigade\" - <i>somehow</i> - the Hulk jumped him presumably miles away from NYC - more <i>ugh</i>!&nbsp; He summons \"Giant-Man\" rather than \"Ant-Man\" (even though at the beginning of the issue, the Avengers call him Ant-Man with no correction from him, but now, even though the Hulk and Iron Man are only a few minutes separated from Rick Jones being left alone, somehow the events of <i>Tales to Astonish</i> #49 have taken place (there's even a blurb in the issue referring to it).&nbsp; Rick Jones has nevertheless found a nearby member of the Teen Brigade and is <i>also</i> aware that he is \"Giant-Man.\"&nbsp; (I have no speculation on how the inconsistencies in this book ever made it out to print!)\n\nWhen Giant-Man and the Wasp join the fray, Iron Man calls him \"Giant-Man\" - so somehow he too has become aware of [LINK:1963:TTA_49_giant0|TTA #49].&nbsp; In the last panel of page 14, the Hulk calls Iron Man \"shell-head.\"&nbsp; This is the first time I remember reading that particular nickname for Iron Man.&nbsp; It's possible that this is its origin point.\n\nWhen Space Phantom assumes the form of Giant-Man, literally as he's disappearing, Hank says \"I'm whirling into another dimension...a shadowy, silent world!\"&nbsp; Wow, he sure can process info fast - we can still see the bottom half of his body in the panel!\n\nMore pages of fighting follow, and Jan runs to Dr. Don Blake, whom she's been told can summon Thor.&nbsp; He sends her out of his office, stamps his cane on the ground to become Thor and <i>immediately</i> calls her back into the office, with Thor there instead of Blake - <i>seriously?</i>  How dumb does he think she is?&nbsp; After filling him in, Thor is already calling out \"Death to the Space Phantom!\"&nbsp; <i>Wow!</i>\n\nThor arrives and deduces that the figure of Iron Man is really the Space Phantom, and calling down torrential rain and lightning, he <i>rusts the Iron Man armor</i> in seconds!&nbsp; (<i>Holy cow!&nbsp; </i>This would seem to be a serious design flaw in the armor suit, and I cannot believe Stark would really be that dumb.)&nbsp; But the Space Phantom then chooses to assume the form of Thor, but he has miscalculated - Thor is not a normal human, so the Space Phantom goes to limbo instead and vanishes.&nbsp; The real Iron Man returns (and un-rusted).\n\nAs the dust settles and they talk about the fighting they did amongst themselves, the Hulk declares that they all hate him deep down and jumps off, quitting the team just like that!&nbsp; What will the world do now without the Avengers to keep the Hulk in check?",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "this was the first time I've dived deep into this issue and realized how pathetic it really is.&nbsp; It was an absolute disaster when it comes to consistency and a dreadful follow-up issue to the solid-enough first one.&nbsp; It was so full of plot holes that if the story itself had been even decent, they would have ruined any enjoyment.&nbsp; (You can kind of see that happening in my various knee-jerk reactions in the summary text above - good god!)&nbsp; The only redeeming quality in my mind that prevented this issue from getting an even lower grade was the novelty of having the Hulk quit the group in only the second issue - something I'm sure nobody saw coming.&nbsp; I don't know if this was the plan all along by Lee and Kirby or if it was something they decided on later in the couple of months of real time between issues 1 and 2 to think about and decide that the Hulk could never really be a good member (not to mention the fact that they already had Thor and Iron Man for brute strength, and now Giant-Man joins too - the Hulk seems expendable no matter what way you choose to look at it).\n\nBut the gaping inconsistencies in this book completely sabotages it.&nbsp; Just...downright...terrible!&nbsp; Having done some writing of my own, I know how deep into the weeds of a story the author can get - in this case writing panel-by-panel.&nbsp; You can easily lose track of the larger matter of how little real time has elapsed.&nbsp; And this story just completely forgets the fact that it (the whole story) elapses in seemingly an hour or less.&nbsp; No way all these other side events could have taken place at the same time!&nbsp; Just no way!\n\nThis is definitely in the top-five (\"bottom\" five?) worst Silver Age Marvels ever, being only slightly less horrible than two others that immediately spring to mind -the Ant-Man story involving the radioactive bug that tries to rule the world, and Iron Man's bout with that other \"Doctor Strange\" - both of those are indeed even worse.&nbsp; But this one ranks right \"down\" there with those and is by far the one riddled with the most plot holes.&nbsp; It was tough to read this!\n\nThe Hulk is clearly shown on the splash page as having only three toes on both feet (and there's absolutely no doubt about the count here - it's quite obvious).&nbsp; I never noticed this before now, so I looked at the previous issue of Avengers to compare.&nbsp; Kirby doesn't provide any close-ups on the feet in that issue, but you can tell he has at least 4 present, and going back even farther, in Incredible [LINK:1962:HULK_5_hulk1|Hulk #5], the opening panel of the second feature story, Kirby draws him with 5 toes, and in #6, Steve Ditko draws at least one panel where you can see 5 toes as well, so while no one really provides a great close-up, in all depictions before this one where you get at least some close-up on the feet, the Hulk is drawn with more than 3, so Jack Kirby seems to have gotten confused here!&nbsp; Not only that, but even in this very issue, Kirby seems to go back and forth between 3 and 4 toes per foot.&nbsp; Don't believe me?&nbsp; Look at the last panel on page 9 and compare to the first one on page 10!&nbsp; It does seem like for the most part, he draws him with 4.&nbsp; But enough of this...!&nbsp; (Except to say that this was called out in Roger Gilman's letter, printed in Avengers #5 as well, to which the response was a call-out to the readership to vote on how many toes \"ol' green skin\" should have.)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI'll at least give Kirby some love for the Space Phantom's appearance - that guy can sure draw memorable villains!\n\nWe see yet another alien invasion plot - thwarted of course by the Avengers.",
  "side_note": [
    "[ASSET: right | NextIssue | width=12%] Note Iron Man's head on this full-page ad for next issue.&nbsp; (And then look at the cover for next issue and see if that's how he looks.)"
